{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "prop:curve-decision-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For any admissible interval band and any finite parameter set \\(F\\subset\\mathcal U\\), let \\(A_F\\) be the random subset of points in \\(F\\) whose entire curves are covered. Then \\(c(A_F)=\\max_{u,u'\\in A_F}d_\\Psi(u,u')\\) is no larger than the band's maximum width, and each grid-point coverage probability is preserved. Conversely, every measurable rule selecting a subset \\(A\\subseteq F\\) induces the pointwise envelope band of those selected curves, with maximum width exactly \\(c(A)\\), and padding this envelope by the uniform \\(u\\)-modulus transports coverage from a finite net to \\(\\mathcal U\\). For each pair \\((u,u')\\), \\(d_\\Psi(u,u')\\) is the maximum of finitely many second-order-cone programs obtained by intersecting the two critical-cone cell systems with \\(\\mathcal D\\). These finite-set reductions apply to every randomized measurable interval-section band and do not impose a nonrandom-shape restriction.",
    "depends_on": [
      "def:local-band-class",
      "lem:local-risk-map"
    ],
    "justification": "This reduction replaces an infinite-dimensional band action by a confidence-set decision under a computable geometry-specific diameter loss.",
    "gap": "FreybergerRai2018UniformBands, Definition 1, restricts bands to those induced by a nonrandom-shape set and rules out randomly selecting among valid bands. Its Section 2.3 leads with weighted area; Theorem 2 is conditional on a generic objective, while the paper supplies low-level sufficient conditions and a worked approximation specifically for weighted area. The separate unresolved target here is the externally randomized max-curve-diameter decision problem over all measurable interval-section bands. EvansHansenStark2005MinimaxConfidence instead uses additive set measure rather than curve diameter.",
    "consumer": "A numerical solver can compute exact pairwise curve distances with primal and dual conic certificates.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix finite \\(F\\subset\\mathcal U\\) and one realization of an admissible band \\(C\\).  Define\n\\[\n \\mathcal A_F(C)=\n \\{u\\in F:\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t)\\in C(t)\\ \\text{for every }t\\in\\mathcal D\\},\n \\qquad\n \\mathcal L(C)=\\sup_{t\\in\\mathcal D}\\operatorname{diam}C(t).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nThese events are measurable by Definition \\(\\mathrm{def:local\\mbox{-}band\\mbox{-}class}\\).  If \\(u,u'\\in\\mathcal A_F(C)\\), both curve values lie in \\(C(t)\\), so\n\\[\n |\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t)-\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u',t)|\n \\leq\\operatorname{diam}C(t).\n\\]\nTaking the relevant maxima proves\n\\[\n c\\{\\mathcal A_F(C)\\}\\leq\\mathcal L(C).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(u\\in\\mathcal A_F(C)\\) is exactly the event that the entire curve indexed by \\(u\\) is covered.  Thus every grid-point coverage probability, including external randomization, is preserved.\n\nConversely, let a randomized measurable rule select \\(A\\subseteq F\\).  For \\(A\\neq\\varnothing\\), set\n\\[\n C_A(t)=\n \\left[\\min_{u\\in A}\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t),\n       \\max_{u\\in A}\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t)\\right].\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\nThe finite extrema are measurable and every selected curve is covered.  Finiteness permits interchange of maxima:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\sup_t\\operatorname{diam}C_A(t)\n &=\\sup_t\\max_{u,u'\\in A}\n |\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t)-\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u',t)|\\\\\n &=\\max_{u,u'\\in A}\\sup_t\n |\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t)-\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u',t)|\n =c_A.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nFor \\(A=\\varnothing\\), take a fixed singleton interval; its width and \\(c_A\\) are zero.  Accidental coverage can only improve validity.  Thus no nonrandom-shape restriction has been introduced.\n\nIf \\(F=\\mathcal U_N\\) is a \\(\\delta\\)-net, choose a measurable nearest point \\(u_{i(u)}\\) with deterministic tie breaking.  Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:local\\mbox{-}risk\\mbox{-}map}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\sup_t|\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u,t)-\\Psi_{\\theta_0}(u_{i(u)},t)|\n \\leq K_u\\delta.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nPadding each nonempty envelope by \\(K_u\\delta\\) at both endpoints makes selection of \\(u_{i(u)}\\) cover the curve at \\(u\\), and increases maximum width by at most \\(2K_u\\delta\\).  This is the deterministic net transport; the associated probability loss is exactly the total-variation term in Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm:certified\\mbox{-}gaussian\\mbox{-}value}\\).\n\nFor computability, use the finitely many closed polyhedral cells from Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:local\\mbox{-}risk\\mbox{-}map}\\).  For fixed \\(u,u'\\) and a pair of cells, their common restrictions on \\(t\\) have the form\n\\[\n \\mathcal T_{ab}=\n \\{t:A_{ab}t\\leq b_{ab},\\\n \\mathbf1^\\prime t=0,\\\n t_k\\geq0\\ \\text{if }(v_0)_k=0,\\\n \\lVert t\\rVert_2\\leq L_0\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nOn this set the curve difference is \\(\\beta_{ab}+\\gamma_{ab}^\\prime t\\).  Its absolute maximum is the larger of\n\\[\n \\max_t\\ \\sigma(\\beta_{ab}+\\gamma_{ab}^\\prime t)\n \\quad\\text{subject to }t\\in\\mathcal T_{ab},\n \\qquad \\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nThese are second-order-cone programs because the only nonlinear constraint is the Euclidean-norm bound.  Compactness ensures attainment.  Maximizing over the finitely many nonempty cell pairs and both signs gives exactly \\(d_\\Psi(u,u')\\).  Uniqueness of the strongly convex critical-cone minimizer makes formulas agree on shared boundaries, so closed-cell enumeration loses no boundary value.  This proves all assertions.",
